Apple this week announced that macOS 27, also known as macOS Golden Gate, will be released to all users with a compatible Mac on Monday, September 14.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/macOS-27-next-to-MacBook-Pro-Running-macOS-27.jpg" alt="" width="2065" height="1162" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1051479" /><br/>
iOS 27, iPadOS 27, watchOS 27, tvOS 27, and visionOS 27 will be released on the same day.<br/>
<br/>
macOS Golden Gate features Siri AI, design improvements, performance enhancements, and more. A developer beta has been available since June, while a public beta followed in July, and now the update will be widely released on September 14.<br/>
<br/>
macOS Golden Gate is compatible with all Macs with an M1 chip or newer:<ul><li>MacBook Neo (2026)</li><br/>
  <li>MacBook Air with Apple silicon (2020 and later)</li><br/>
  <li>MacBook Pro with Apple silicon (2020 and later)</li><br/>
  <li>iMac (2021 and later)</li><br/>
  <li>Mac mini (2020 and later)</li><br/>
  <li>Mac Studio (2022 and later)</li><br/>
  <li>Mac Pro (2023 and later)</li><br/>
</ul>macOS 26 aka macOS Tahoe is the final macOS release compatible with Intel-based Macs.<br/>
<br/>
Intel Macs that can run macOS Tahoe, but not macOS Golden Gate:<ul><li>13-inch MacBook Pro (2020, Four Thunderbolt 3 Ports)</li><br/>
  <li>16-inch MacBook Pro (2019)</li><br/>
  <li>27-inch iMac (2020)</li><br/>
  <li>Mac Pro (2019)</li></ul>Apple said Intel-based Macs will continue to receive security updates for three years.<br/>
<br/>
